---
description: Spec and task file conventions
globs: ["specs/**", "tasks/**"]
alwaysApply: false
---

# Specs (`specs/`)

- `specs/SYSTEM.md` is the living entry point: business goal, architecture
  overview, a module index and a requirement index, each with current
  status. Update it when a change shifts a module's or requirement's status.
- One spec per module at `specs/modules/<name>.md`; product-level specs at `specs/`.
- Use the template in `specs/README.md`: Purpose · Requirement refs · Public interface
  (capabilities, events, API routes) · Data owned · Behavior · Acceptance criteria ·
  Out of scope.
- Every spec cites the build-doc sections it derives from
  (`docs/TenderShield_Full_Build_Doc.md`). Anything beyond the doc is marked
  `assumption:`.
- Specs and code must never contradict; fix the mismatch in the same change.
- Requirement docs (`specs/requirements/R-0xx-*.md`) stay business/behavior-level —
  Purpose, target behavior, acceptance criteria — and name which task(s) implement
  them. Code-level detail (snippets, `file:line`, files touched) belongs in the
  task's own file (see below), not blended into the requirement doc.

# Tasks (`tasks/TRACKER.md` + `tasks/specs/`)

- **One master tracker: `tasks/TRACKER.md`.** Every task ever created gets exactly
  one row there, sectioned by phase/gate. This replaced four separate tracker files
  (`backlog.md`, `gap_remediation_tracker.md`, `phase15_tracker.md`,
  `spec_audit_tracker.md`), which are now stubs pointing here — do not create a new
  tracker file; add a section to this one instead.
- **One task file per task: `tasks/specs/TS-###-<slug>.md`.** Template and
  conventions in `tasks/specs/README.md`. Code-level: current vs. target code with
  `file:line`, files touched, tests, and the acceptance criteria this task closes.
  Every task — done or not — gets one, including tasks predating the requirement-doc
  system (reconstructed from current code + the relevant module spec).
- IDs are sequential `TS-###`, never reused.
- Statuses: `todo | in-progress | blocked | done`.
- A task is not closeable without *both* its tracker row set to `done` *and* its
  task file completed (code shipped, tests listed, commit hash).
- Run `python scripts/check_tracker.py` before pushing — it verifies every tracker
  row's task-file and requirement links resolve, and reports the live done/left
  count. It fails (exit 1) on a broken link, duplicate ID, or bad status value; a
  task still missing its file is reported as backlog, not a failure — unless it's
  the task you were just asked to close.
- Order new work by build value (Build Doc §13.5) and by the gate structure already
  in `tasks/TRACKER.md`. Don't pull later-gate tasks ahead of an earlier gate's P0s.
